const unit2 = {"id": 2, "title": "Assessing nutritional health", "subtitle": "Assessment, reference values and nourishing choices", "lessons": [["Nutritional status", "Follow intake, absorption, use and losses.", "<p>The central question is: <b>How do we know whether someone is getting the nutrition their body needs?</b> Unit 1 introduced nutrients and nutrition science. Unit 2 connects those foundations to assessment, recommendations and food choices.</p><h3>More than what you eat</h3><p>Nutritional status describes the body\u2019s condition in relation to the nutrients and energy it receives and uses. Follow the whole process:</p><ul><li><b>Intake:</b> Is enough consumed?</li><li><b>Digestion:</b> Can food be broken down?</li><li><b>Absorption:</b> Can nutrients move from the intestine into the body?</li><li><b>Use:</b> Can tissues metabolize them properly?</li><li><b>Losses and requirements:</b> Are losses or needs increased?</li></ul><div class=\"example\"><b>Same intake, different status</b><p>Two people may eat similar amounts of iron but differ in iron status because of blood loss or absorption. Food intake and nutritional status are related, but they are not interchangeable.</p></div><p class=\"muted\">This is the teaching overview developed in our conversation, supported by your Lesson Test 2 and authoritative references. The Chapter 2 text was not supplied, so this is not verified coverage of every section in the textbook edition.</p>"], ["Malnutrition", "Understand deficiency, excess and underlying causes.", "<p>Malnutrition is an imbalance involving energy or nutrients. It includes both <b>undernutrition and overnutrition</b>.</p><table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Undernutrition</td><td>Insufficient energy or nutrients</td><td>Insufficient energy and protein over time</td></tr><tr><td>Overnutrition</td><td>Excess energy or particular nutrients</td><td>Excessive supplement intake causing harm</td></tr><tr><td>Primary malnutrition</td><td>Problem begins with inadequate or excessive intake</td><td>Limited food access reduces intake</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary malnutrition</td><td>A condition affects absorption, use or losses</td><td>Intestinal disease reduces absorption</td></tr></table><h3>Categories can overlap</h3><p>A person may consume excess energy while having inadequate iron or vitamin D. Body size alone cannot establish nutrient adequacy.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>After gastric bypass</b><p>Changes to the digestive system can contribute to inadequate absorption and intake. Malnutrition related to these changes is classified as secondary. The important question is what is preventing needs from being met.</p></div>"], ["The ABCD assessment", "Combine body measurements, labs, clinical findings and diet.", "<table><tr><th>Assessment</th><th>Examines</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>A \u2014 Anthropometric</td><td>Body size and composition</td><td>Height, weight, waist and weight change</td></tr><tr><td>B \u2014 Biochemical</td><td>Laboratory measurements</td><td>Blood and urine markers</td></tr><tr><td>C \u2014 Clinical</td><td>History, signs and symptoms</td><td>Physical examination, appetite and digestion</td></tr><tr><td>D \u2014 Dietary</td><td>Food, drink and supplements</td><td>Records, recalls and eating patterns</td></tr></table><p>Each is one part of the picture. Low reported iron intake does not diagnose deficiency. Laboratory results require interpretation in context; illness and inflammation can affect some markers.</p><h3>Weight and body composition</h3><p>Weight is total mass. Body composition describes fat, lean tissues, water and bone mineral. These components are not all separate in every model: lean tissue includes substantial water.</p><p><b>BMI = weight (kg) \u00f7 height (m)\u00b2.</b> BMI is a screening measure, not a direct measure of body fat, nutrient adequacy or individual health. Trends, such as unintentional weight loss, can matter even when BMI stays in a usual range.</p><h3>Signs versus symptoms</h3><p>A <b>sign</b> is observed or measured, such as swelling or a visible rash. A <b>symptom</b> is experienced and reported, such as nausea, fatigue or loss of appetite. Some problems involve both: breathlessness can be reported while breathing difficulty may also be observed.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Combine the clues</b><p>A client reports fatigue and low appetite (clinical), a record suggests low intake (dietary), weight has fallen (anthropometric), and a clinician interprets relevant labs (biochemical). No single clue tells the whole story.</p></div>"], ["Dietary assessment methods", "Understand what each method can and cannot tell you.", "<table><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Limitation</th></tr><tr><td>24-hour recall</td><td>Reconstructs yesterday\u2019s intake</td><td>Memory and portion estimation</td></tr><tr><td>Food frequency questionnaire</td><td>Reports how often listed foods are eaten over a longer period</td><td>Limited list and less detail</td></tr><tr><td>Diet history</td><td>Explores usual meals and habits</td><td>Reporting and interview quality</td></tr><tr><td>Food record</td><td>Records intake as consumed over several days</td><td>Burden and behaviour changes</td></tr></table><h3>Retrospective versus prospective</h3><p><b>Retrospective</b> looks back: \u201cWhat did you eat yesterday?\u201d <b>Prospective</b> records forward: \u201cRecord what you eat over the next three days.\u201d</p><p>A recall uses follow-up questions about portions, preparation and forgotten foods. A single day may not represent usual intake. Multiple days can capture variation.</p><h3>A useful food record</h3><p>Include portions, brands when relevant, preparation, drinks, sauces, cooking oils and supplements. Different types of days, including a weekend day, can reveal variation. Three days is a common teaching example, not a universal rule.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Add the details</b><p>\u201cChicken sandwich\u201d is less informative than \u201ctwo slices of bread, chicken breast, mayonnaise and cheese,\u201d with quantities. Record as you eat to reduce reliance on memory.</p></div><p>No self-reported method is perfectly accurate. People can forget items or estimate portions incorrectly. They may also change their eating because they are recording it: <b>reactivity</b>. Food records are not universally the most accurate method for every purpose.</p><p><a href=\"https://epi.grants.cancer.gov/dietary-assessment-primer/profiles/recall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NCI: 24-hour recall</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://epi.grants.cancer.gov/dietary-assessment-primer/profiles/record/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NCI: food records</a></p>"], ["Nutrient analysis", "Turn food information into useful estimates.", "<p>Food composition tables and computerized databases estimate energy, carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins, minerals and fibre in recorded foods.</p><h3>Input quality matters</h3><p>Choosing the wrong food entry, confusing raw and cooked weights, omitting ingredients, or entering inaccurate portions changes the result.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Raw versus cooked</b><p>A measured amount of dry rice and the same weight of cooked rice have different nutrient concentrations because cooking adds water. Select the entry that matches the food actually measured.</p></div><p><b>Estimated intake is not the same as absorbed intake.</b> Analysis identifies possible gaps to investigate; it does not alone diagnose deficiency.</p>"], ["Requirements and DRIs", "Learn EAR, RDA, AI and UL without mixing them up.", "<p>A <b>requirement</b> is the amount a person needs for a particular nutritional function or health outcome. Needs vary with age, life stage and physiology.</p><p>Because we usually cannot measure each person\u2019s exact requirement, we use <b>Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs)</b>: reference values for healthy populations used in Canada and the United States.</p><table><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>EAR: Estimated Average Requirement</td><td>Meets about 50% of a group\u2019s needs</td><td>Estimated midpoint of requirements</td></tr><tr><td>RDA: Recommended Dietary Allowance</td><td>Meets about 97\u201398% of a group\u2019s needs</td><td>Individual intake goal</td></tr><tr><td>AI: Adequate Intake</td><td>Used when evidence cannot establish an EAR/RDA</td><td>Alternative intake goal</td></tr><tr><td>UL: Tolerable Upper Intake Level</td><td>Highest usual daily intake unlikely to cause harm for almost everyone in a group</td><td>Safety boundary, not a target</td></tr></table><h3>How to interpret them</h3><ul><li>Below the RDA does not automatically mean deficiency; the RDA covers almost everyone, including higher requirements.</li><li>Above the RDA does not automatically provide extra benefit.</li><li>Below an AI does not by itself establish inadequacy; it is not a known requirement cutoff.</li><li>Above a UL raises concern about adverse effects, but does not mean toxicity immediately occurs.</li></ul><p>Duration, amount and individual circumstances matter. Some ULs apply to particular forms or sources. Check table notes. References concern <b>usual intake over time</b>, not whether every isolated day meets every value.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Above RDA, below UL</b><p>For a healthy individual in the appropriate group, this suggests usual intake is probably adequate and below the established upper boundary. It does not prove extra benefit.</p></div><h3>Why standards developed</h3><p>Early RDAs primarily aimed to prevent deficiencies. Modern reference systems also consider broader health outcomes. EAR, RDA, AI and UL are the four core categories taught here; energy and macronutrient guidance add other reference concepts.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada DRI definitions and tables</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/definitions-relationship-risk-inadequacy-risk-adverse-effects.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Interpreting risk and reference values</a></p>"], ["Estimated energy needs", "Understand the estimate, the units and the coursework equation.", "<p>An <b>Estimated Energy Requirement (EER)</b> estimates energy intake needed to support energy balance for a person with specified characteristics. Energy expenditure includes resting functions, activity, processing food and additional needs in growth or certain life stages.</p><p>Age, body size and activity influence the estimate. An equation is a starting point, not a precise measurement of individual needs. Energy equations were updated in 2023; this quiz appears to use the older equations below.</p><h3>Keep units consistent</h3><p>kg \u2248 pounds \u00f7 2.205 (coursework often simplifies to \u00f7 2.2). The older equations use height in <b>metres</b>, weight in <b>kilograms</b>, age in years and return kcal/day.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Older adult EER equations used in coursework</b><p>Men: 662 \u2212 (9.53 \u00d7 age) + PA \u00d7 [(15.91 \u00d7 weight) + (539.6 \u00d7 height)]</p><p>Women: 354 \u2212 (6.91 \u00d7 age) + PA \u00d7 [(9.36 \u00d7 weight) + (726 \u00d7 height)]</p></div><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Older male PA</th><th>Older female PA</th></tr><tr><td>Sedentary</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td></tr><tr><td>Low active</td><td>1.11</td><td>1.12</td></tr><tr><td>Active</td><td>1.25</td><td>1.27</td></tr><tr><td>Very active</td><td>1.48</td><td>1.45</td></tr></table><p>PA is a coefficient specific to these equations. It is not the same thing as a generic activity multiplier for every energy equation. Activity reflects the whole day, not just exercise sessions.</p><h3>Worked example with different numbers</h3><p>For a hypothetical 30-year-old man, 70 kg, 1.75 m, sedentary:</p><p>662 \u2212 (9.53 \u00d7 30) + 1.00 \u00d7 [(15.91 \u00d7 70) + (539.6 \u00d7 1.75)]<br>= 662 \u2212 285.90 + 1,113.70 + 944.30<br>= <b>2,434.10 kcal/day</b>.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/food-nutrition-surveillance/health-nutrition-surveys/canadian-community-health-survey-cchs/canadian-community-health-survey-cycle-2-2-nutrition-2004-guide-accessing-interpreting-data-health-canada-2006.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada historical equations</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://health-infobase.canada.ca/nutrition/dietary-reference-intakes-calculator/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Current Health Canada calculator</a></p>"], ["Macronutrient balance", "Convert grams to energy before calculating percentages.", "<p>The <b>Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR)</b> expresses recommended intake as a percentage of energy.</p><table><tr><th>Adult macronutrient</th><th>AMDR</th></tr><tr><td>Carbohydrate</td><td>45\u201365%</td></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>20\u201335%</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>10\u201335%</td></tr></table><p>These are broad guidance, not a complete individualized plan. Convert grams to kcal first: carbohydrate and protein approximately 4 kcal/g; fat approximately 9 kcal/g.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Worked example</b><p>A 2,000-kcal diet with 250 g carbohydrate supplies 250 \u00d7 4 = 1,000 carbohydrate kcal. Then 1,000 \u00f7 2,000 \u00d7 100 = <b>50% of energy</b>.</p></div><p>Grams alone cannot tell you the energy percentage. A gram of fat supplies more energy than a gram of carbohydrate or protein. Use the Energy workshop to practise.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-macronutrients.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health Canada macronutrient reference table</a></p>"], ["Nutrient and energy density", "Compare foods using the right denominator.", "<p><b>Nutrient density</b> describes useful nutrients relative to energy. For a particular nutrient: <b>nutrient amount \u00f7 kcal</b>.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Same vitamin, different energy</b><p>Food A supplies 60 mg vitamin C and 60 kcal: 1 mg/kcal. Food B supplies 60 mg and 120 kcal: 0.5 mg/kcal. A supplies twice as much vitamin C per calorie.</p></div><p><b>Energy density</b> means kcal per gram of food. It is a different measure. Nuts can be both nutrient-dense and energy-dense.</p><h3>Compare the actual foods</h3><p>Added syrup or sugar often adds energy without comparable amounts of the nutrients being considered. A fresh fruit may therefore have greater nutrient density than its preserve or dessert version, but the specific recipe and nutrient matter.</p><p>A calcium-rich food is not necessarily vitamin-C-rich. Assess the overall eating pattern rather than expecting every food to provide everything. Lower energy is not automatically the right goal for every person.</p>"], ["Nutrition labels and Daily Values", "Scale servings and distinguish %DV from energy percentage.", "<p>Start with <b>serving size</b>. Label amounts refer to that amount, which may differ from your portion. If one serving provides 5 g fibre and you eat two servings, you consume 10 g.</p><p><b>Daily Value (DV)</b> is a standardized labeling reference, not necessarily an individual requirement.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>Three different ideas</b><p>%DV = nutrient amount \u00f7 label Daily Value \u00d7 100.<br>% of energy = nutrient kcal \u00f7 total kcal \u00d7 100.<br>RDA = nutrient intake recommendation for a particular group.</p></div><h3>The supplied cereal label</h3><img class=\"sourceimg\" src=\"unit2-label.png\" alt=\"Original cereal Nutrition Facts label: serving size 1 cup, fibre 8 g or 32% DV, sugars 19 g; older daily values include carbohydrate 300 g and fibre 25 g.\"><p>The source uses older U.S. reference values: carbohydrate 300 g and fibre 25 g. Current U.S. Daily Values include carbohydrate 275 g and fibre 28 g. For coursework using the printed label, use its printed values.</p><h3>Work from the serving multiplier</h3><p>Actual portion \u00f7 label serving size = number of servings. Multiply nutrient grams and printed %DV by this factor.</p><div class=\"example\"><b>A separate practice example</b><p>If \u00bd cup contains 3 g fibre, then 1 cup is two servings and contains 6 g fibre. If its label says 12% DV per serving, two servings provide 24% DV.</p></div><h3>Sugar equivalents</h3><p>Approximately 4 g sugar equals one teaspoon. Multiply by servings first, then divide sugar grams by four. Total sugar includes naturally occurring and added sugars; this conversion does not mean all the sugar was added.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-facts-label/daily-value-nutrition-and-supplement-facts-labels\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FDA current Daily Values</a></p>"], ["From assessment to a plan", "Make changes that fit nutrient needs and real life.", "<p>Assessment identifies needs so you can choose realistic changes. A pattern low in calcium, vitamin D and protein might be improved with fortified milk or an appropriately fortified soy beverage. Products differ, so read labels. Not all dairy products are vitamin-D-fortified, and plant beverages vary in protein and fortification.</p><p>A useful plan considers preferences, culture, allergies, budget, access, digestion and routines alongside nutrient needs.</p><ol><li>Gather evidence from several assessment methods.</li><li>Identify possible gaps or imbalances.</li><li>Compare usual intake with appropriate references.</li><li>Choose feasible changes.</li><li>Reassess intake and relevant outcomes over time.</li></ol><div class=\"example\"><b>The connection to remember</b><p>Food intake provides clues. Assessment helps interpret the clues. Reference values guide planning. No single record, measurement or reference number tells the whole story.</p></div><h3>Your recap</h3><p>Explain primary versus secondary malnutrition. Name the ABCD methods. Distinguish EAR, RDA, AI and UL. Describe retrospective versus prospective assessment. Calculate energy percentages and serving multipliers. Distinguish nutrient density, energy density and %DV.</p><p>When you feel ready, open Unit 2 Practice. The explanations identify older coursework assumptions and ambiguous wording without treating them as current individualized advice.</p>"]], "questions": [{"n": 1, "q": "Undernutrition and overnutrition both are examples of ______________________________", "o": ["malnutrition", "nutritional deficiency", "nutritional toxicity", "overconsumption", "underconsumption"], "a": 0, "e": "Both inadequate and excessive nutrition fall under malnutrition.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 2, "q": "Mrs. Jones is recovering from gastric bypass surgery, and as a result has become deficient in a number of nutrients. Which type of malnutrition is Mrs. Jones experiencing?", "o": ["Primary", "Secondary", "Post-surgical", "Multiple state"], "a": 1, "e": "Surgical changes affecting intake or absorption are an underlying cause: secondary malnutrition.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 3, "q": "Estimates of physical aspects of the body such as body composition are categorized as ___________________ measurements.", "o": ["anthropometric", "biochemical", "clinical", "dietary"], "a": 0, "e": "Anthropometric assessment concerns body size, proportions and composition.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 4, "q": "An estimate of your _____________________, the proportions of lean tissue, body fat, water and mineral (bone) mass can help determine your nutritional status.", "o": ["body weight", "body height", "body composition", "body mass index"], "a": 2, "e": "Body composition describes the makeup of body mass; weight and BMI alone do not.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 5, "q": "A physical examination during nutritional assessment is considered a(n)", "o": ["anthropometric measurement", "biochemical assessment", "clinical assessment", "dietary assessment"], "a": 2, "e": "A physical examination belongs to clinical assessment.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 6, "q": "Of the following, which would be considered a symptom?", "o": ["Loss of appetite", "Swollen ankles", "Skin rash", "Difficulty breathing"], "a": 0, "e": "Loss of appetite is a subjective symptom. Difficulty breathing can also be a reported symptom or an observed sign, making the question ambiguous. A is likely intended; this item is unscored.", "unscored": true, "image": null}, {"n": 7, "q": "A _____________________________________ is a retrospective dietary assessment method which asks a person to quantify how often and in what amounts they eat or drink a specific food over a period of time.", "o": ["7-day diet record", "24-hour recall", "diet history", "food frequency questionnaire"], "a": 3, "e": "A food frequency questionnaire asks about frequency and sometimes portion sizes over a retrospective period.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 8, "q": "An example of a prospective method of dietary assessment would be a:", "o": ["24-hour recall", "food frequency questionnaire", "diet history", "3-day diet record"], "a": 3, "e": "A 3-day food record records future intake as it happens: prospective.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 9, "q": "Which method of assessing dietary intake is most accurate and requires food and beverages to be recorded as they are consumed?", "o": ["diet record", "24-hour recall", "diet history", "food frequency questionnaire"], "a": 0, "e": "A diet record is the intended answer because it records intake as consumed. The claim \u201cmost accurate\u201d is too broad: error and reactivity remain, and the best method depends on purpose.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 10, "q": "Which of the following is not true about diet records?", "o": ["Food portion sizes must be estimated using standard household measurements (such as tablespoons or cups) or weighed prior to consumption", "Food records may be conducted by medical professionals or as self-assessments", "A food record\u2019s accuracy depends on the individual\u2019s memory", "Food records should be kept for 3 days, one which should be a weekend day"], "a": 2, "e": "Recording intake as consumed reduces reliance on memory. Three days including a weekend day is a common protocol, not a mandatory duration for all records.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 11, "q": "What method is considered easy to use should you wish to determine the micronutrient, macronutrient, and energy (calorie) content of your diet?", "o": ["Food composition tables", "Computerized nutrient databases", "Dietary Reference Intakes", "Nutrition Fact labels"], "a": 1, "e": "Computerized nutrient databases make nutrient and energy calculations easier, provided the inputs and food entries are appropriate.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 12, "q": "The RDAs were originally designed primarily to:", "o": ["prevent nutrient deficiencies", "reduce risk of chronic disease", "correct nutrient deficiencies", "prevent overnutrition"], "a": 0, "e": "Early RDAs primarily aimed to prevent deficiencies, rather than treat established deficiency or prevent excess.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 13, "q": "\"____ \" is a term used to describe a set of four groups of values: EAR, RDAs, AIs, and ULs.", "o": ["Daily Reference Intakes (DRIs)", "Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs)", "Reference Daily Intakes (RDIs)", "Reference Dietary Intakes (RDIs)"], "a": 1, "e": "DRI stands for Dietary Reference Intakes.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 14, "q": "A nutrient_______________________ is the amount of a nutrient that must be consumed to prevent deficiencies; it is determined by a person\u2019s nutritional needs based on a host of factors such as sex, age, physical activity, and genetics.", "o": ["status", "requirement", "adequacy", "intake"], "a": 1, "e": "A nutrient requirement describes the amount needed for a specified function or outcome; it varies among individuals.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 15, "q": "The ______ for a particular nutrient is the intake value thought to meet the requirement of half the healthy individuals of the same gender who are in a particular stage of life.", "o": ["Estimated Average Requirement (EAR)", "Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)", "Recommended Daily Allowance (RDA)", "Adequate Intake Level (AI)"], "a": 0, "e": "The EAR meets the requirements of approximately half of healthy individuals in the specified group.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 16, "q": "The _________ provide limits for those who take supplements or consume large amounts of fortified foods.", "o": ["Estimated Average Requirements (EARs)", "Tolerable Upper Intake Levels (ULs)", "Adequate Intakes (AIs)", "Recommended Dietary Allowances (RDAs)"], "a": 1, "e": "ULs are upper boundaries for usual intake. Check the relevant source/form restrictions for each nutrient.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 17, "q": "A 25-year-old woman has recently visited a registered dietitian as part of a routine health exam. She is consuming more than the RDA, yet less than the UL, for nutrient x. Her intake for that nutrient is:", "o": ["probably inadequate.", "possibly inadequate.", "probably adequate.", "more than adequate.", "None of the above."], "a": 2, "e": "Usual intake above the RDA but below the UL is probably adequate for the relevant healthy group; extra benefit is not implied.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 18, "q": "If intake of a nutrient in the diet falls below the AI, then:", "o": ["more information is needed to determine the adequacy of diet.", "the diet is inadequate.", "the diet is poor.", "the diet is probably adequate."], "a": 0, "e": "An AI is not a known minimum requirement. Below it, additional information is needed to assess adequacy.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 19, "q": "Which of the following physical activity factors would be appropriate to use when calculating the EER for a sedentary man and/or woman?", "o": ["1.0", "1.11", "1.25", "1.48"], "a": 0, "e": "In the older coursework equations, sedentary PA = 1.00 for both adult men and women. This coefficient is specific to those equations.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 20, "q": "A female college student who weighs 110 lbs. would weigh ________ kilograms.", "o": ["43", "45", "50", "55"], "a": 2, "e": "110 \u00f7 2.2 = 50 kg. Using 2.205 gives 49.89 kg, still approximately 50 kg.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 21, "q": "What is the Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR) for carbohydrates, as a percentage of total energy?", "o": ["40%-60%", "45%-60%", "45%-65%", "50%-65%"], "a": 2, "e": "The adult carbohydrate AMDR is 45\u201365% of energy.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 22, "q": "Mathew is a 25 year old male who weighs 175 pounds. He is 1.88m tall, and has an active activity level. What would Mathew's EER be? When calculating use 2 decimal places (where applicable).", "o": ["3,474 kcal", "3,020 kcal", "3,274 kcal", "None of the above"], "a": 2, "e": "Using the older adult male equation and the coursework conversion: 175 \u00f7 2.2 = 79.55 kg. EER = 662 \u2212 9.53\u00d725 + 1.25\u00d7(15.91\u00d779.55 + 539.6\u00d71.88) = 3273.86 kcal/day, approximately 3274. Using 2.205 for conversion gives about 3270 kcal; C remains the intended choice. Current energy equations differ.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 23, "q": "You want to choose the most nutrient-dense food to improve your vitamin C intake. You are deciding between 3\u20444 cup of fresh orange juice (84 kcal, 93 mg vitamin C), 1 cup of fresh pink grapefruit juice (96 kcal, 94 mg of vitamin C), and 1 cup canned apple juice (117 kcal, 2 mg vitamin C). Which is the best choice?", "o": ["Orange juice", "Grapefruit juice", "Apple juice", "All are equal"], "a": 0, "e": "Compare vitamin C per kcal: orange 93\u00f784 = 1.11 mg/kcal; grapefruit 94\u00f796 = 0.98; apple 2\u00f7117 = 0.017. Orange juice has the highest vitamin C density among these portions.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 24, "q": "Which one of the following food items is the most nutrient dense?", "o": ["Fresh peaches", "Canned peaches in syrup", "Peach pie", "Peach preserves"], "a": 0, "e": "Fresh peaches are the intended comparison: syrup, pie and preserves commonly add energy without proportional micronutrient increases. Exact density depends on the nutrient and recipe.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 25, "q": "If the results of a nutritional analysis revealed an inadequate intake of calcium, vitamin D, and protein, increased intakes of which one of the following food groups, by itself, could help correct this situation?", "o": ["Grains", "Protein foods", "Dairy", "Vegetables"], "a": 2, "e": "Dairy is the intended category: milk can supply protein, calcium and, when fortified, vitamin D. Not every dairy product supplies all three well; fortified alternatives also vary.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 26, "q": "Based on a reference diet of 2,000 Calories, what percentage of the daily value (%DV) for carbohydrate is provided by a 1-cup serving of macaroni and cheese that contains 60 grams of carbohydrate per serving?", "o": ["12", "15", "17", "20"], "a": 3, "e": "This question uses the older U.S. carbohydrate DV of 300 g: 60\u00f7300\u00d7100 = 20%. With the current 275-g U.S. DV it would be about 22%; keep historical and current values distinct.", "unscored": false, "image": null}, {"n": 27, "q": "If you ate a large bowl (~1.5 cups) of the Bran Flakes with Raisins cereal, how many grams of fibre did you consume from the cereal?", "o": ["4 grams", "8 grams", "12 grams", "16 grams"], "a": 2, "e": "The label serving is 1 cup with 8 g fibre. 1.5 cups \u00d7 8 g/cup = 12 g fibre.", "unscored": false, "image": "unit2-label.png"}, {"n": 28, "q": "If you ate a large bowl (~1.5 cups) of the Bran Flakes with Raisins cereal, what % of the Daily Value (%DV) of fibre is being provided based on a 2000-kcal diet?", "o": ["12", "32", "48", "64"], "a": 2, "e": "The source label has 32% DV fibre per cup. 1.5 \u00d7 32% = 48%. Equivalently 12\u00f725\u00d7100 = 48% using its older fibre DV.", "unscored": false, "image": "unit2-label.png"}, {"n": 29, "q": "If there are 4 grams of carbohydrates in a teaspoon of sugar, how many teaspoons of sugar are there in 2 cups of Bran Flakes with Raisins cereal?", "o": ["4.5 teaspoons", "6 teaspoons", "9.5 teaspoons", "11 teaspoons"], "a": 2, "e": "Two cups supply 2\u00d719 = 38 g total sugar. 38\u00f74 = 9.5 teaspoons equivalent; this does not mean all sugar is added.", "unscored": false, "image": "unit2-label.png"}, {"n": 30, "q": "Jodi\u2019s nutritional intake for one day revealed the following: 2010 kcalories, 90 g of fat, 80 g protein, and 220 g of carbohydrate. Jodi\u2019s carbohydrate intake is:", "o": ["44%of total kcalories.", "48% of total kcalories.", "52% of total kcalories.", "56% of total kcalories."], "a": 0, "e": "Using the stated total: 220\u00d74 = 880 kcal from carbohydrate; 880\u00f72010\u00d7100 = 43.78%, approximately 44%. The macro grams sum to 2010 kcal using 4/4/9, so the figures are internally consistent.", "unscored": false, "image": null}], "cards": [["Nutritional status", "The body\u2019s condition in relation to nutrients and energy received and used; intake, absorption, use and losses all matter."], ["Primary versus secondary malnutrition", "Primary starts with intake. Secondary involves an underlying condition affecting intake, absorption, use or losses."], ["ABCD assessment", "Anthropometric, biochemical, clinical and dietary. Combine evidence rather than relying on one clue."], ["Sign versus symptom", "A sign is observed or measured. A symptom is experienced and reported. Some problems involve both."], ["Retrospective versus prospective", "Retrospective looks back (recall, FFQ). Prospective records forward as intake occurs (food record)."], ["Food record reactivity", "People may change their eating because they are recording it."], ["EAR", "Estimated Average Requirement: meets about 50% of the healthy group\u2019s requirements."], ["RDA", "Recommended Dietary Allowance: meets approximately 97\u201398% of the specified healthy group\u2019s requirements."], ["AI", "Adequate Intake: an alternative intake goal when evidence is insufficient for an EAR/RDA. Below AI does not alone prove inadequacy."], ["UL", "Tolerable Upper Intake Level: a usual intake boundary for adverse-effect risk, not a target."], ["EER", "Estimated Energy Requirement: an estimate supporting energy balance. Match units and the equation version."], ["Adult AMDR", "Carbohydrate 45\u201365%, fat 20\u201335%, protein 10\u201335% of energy."], ["Nutrient density versus energy density", "Nutrients relative to energy versus kcal per gram of food. A food can be high in both."], ["%DV versus energy percentage", "%DV compares nutrient amount with a label reference. Energy percentage compares nutrient kcal with total kcal."], ["Serving multiplier", "Actual portion \u00f7 label serving size. Multiply nutrient amounts and printed %DV by this factor."], ["Sugar teaspoons", "Approximately total sugar grams \u00f7 4. This is a total-sugar equivalent, not a measure of added sugar."]]};